(* ============================================================================
   PART 4: ATTRIBUTES
   ============================================================================ *)

attributes = '{', attr_pad, attribute_list, attr_pad, '}' ;
attribute_list = attribute, {space+, attribute} ;
(* interior padding is accepted: `{ .a }` and `{.a  .b}` are valid inline
   attribute blocks. The terminal is `space`, NOT `whitespace`: see
   THE INLINE INTERIOR IS SPACE-ONLY below. block_attributes pads and
   separates differently, and deliberately. *)
(* THE INLINE INTERIOR IS SPACE-ONLY, THE BLOCK-ATTRIBUTE LINE IS NOT --
   NORMATIVE (carve#906). Every whitespace slot of the INLINE block takes
   `space`: the run after `{`, the run between two attributes, the run
   before `}`, the boundary after an unquoted value, and the blessed empty
   block `{ }`. All five sit inline, so no exception has to be written. A TAB
   IS SYNTAX ONLY IN THE LEADING RUN (PART 7).

   `block_attributes` keeps `whitespace` at its three, and that is the
   ruling rather than an oversight. It is the one construct in this grammar
   whose interior can contain a LEADING INDENTATION RUN: after a
   `continuation`, the next line's leading whitespace IS indentation, and
   the rule that narrows the inline block is the same rule that protects
   this one. The distinction is POSITIONAL, not per-construct - inline
   padding is space-only everywhere, and a continuation line's leading run
   is indentation like any other leading run.

   Two neighbouring questions are explicitly NOT decided here and have
   their own tickets: a NEWLINE inside a quoted value, where this file and
   carve-core.ohm split and the engines are 2-1 (carve#888), and
   carve-php capping a block-attribute block at one line break where the
   other two engines and the oracle accept N. *)
(* RENDER ORDER -- NORMATIVE: attributes are emitted in the order they
   appear in the source, with all classes merged into a single `class`
   attribute placed at the position of the FIRST class. So `{.a #b k=c}`
   -> `class="a" id="b" k="c"` and `{k=c .a #b}` -> `k="c" class="a"
   id="b"`. (Block-attribute merge keeps each slot at its first-appearance
   position; values are last-wins, PART 9 §15.) Repeated class VALUES are
   DEDUPLICATED, keeping first-occurrence order: `{.a .a .b}` -> `class="a
   b"`, and classes accumulated across adjacent/continued attribute blocks
   dedup the same way. *)

attribute = language_attribute | id_attribute | class_attribute
          | key_value_attribute | boolean_attribute ;

(* `{:TAG}` is EXACT SUGAR for `key_value_attribute` `lang=TAG`, and the empty
   `{:}` for `lang=""`. It desugars during attribute parsing, so there is no
   new AST node, no new AST field, and a consumer that has never heard of the
   shorthand sees an ordinary `lang` key/value.

   `{:}` is not the same as omitting the attribute. Omitting it leaves the
   content inheriting a surrounding language; `{:}` declares the language
   explicitly unknown and stops that inheritance. It is also distinct from
   `{:und}` (BCP 47 `und`, undetermined) and `{:zxx}` (no linguistic content).

   PARSING CHECKS THE STRUCTURAL ENVELOPE ONLY, never a registry: conformance
   must not depend on the IANA Language Subtag Registry, which changes. So
   subtags are ASCII alphanumerics, one to eight characters, hyphen-separated,
   with no empty subtag and no leading or trailing hyphen. Tags are stored
   EXACTLY as authored -- BCP 47 comparison is case-insensitive but parsing and
   formatting do not change case. A candidate that fails this envelope is not
   a partially consumed attribute: the whole block stays literal (PART 9 §14),
   so `{:en_US}`, `{:en--GB}`, `{:-en}` and `{:français}` are literal source.

   LANGUAGE NEVER IMPLIES WRITING DIRECTION. A tag need not carry a script
   subtag, so deriving direction would require changing CLDR likely-subtag and
   script-direction data and could change unchanged output after a data update.
   No tag produces `dir` in either direction. Authors use HTML's independent
   mechanism explicitly where needed: `[…]{:ar dir=rtl}` or `dir=auto`.

   The deferred braced-symbol spelling `{:name:}` stays literal under this
   production, because a trailing `:` is not a subtag character -- see
   `docs/dismissed-syntax.md`, which reserves `{:` for that form. The two are
   distinguishable (a symbol ends `:}` and a language tag cannot), and that
   entry is amended in the same change rather than read around.

   THE SIGIL TAKES NO PADDING -- NORMATIVE: the tag is optional, not the
   separator, so `{: fr}` is the empty language attribute followed by a
   SEPARATE boolean `fr`, giving `lang="" fr=""`. It is not a language
   attribute reading `fr`, and it does not fail the block. This follows from
   `':', [ language_tag ]` with no special case, and it is the answer a reader
   derives from the production; a rule that made a space here fail the whole
   block would be one the grammar does not say. The cost lands on a typo rather
   than on a shape anyone writes deliberately.

   COLLISION -- NORMATIVE: `:TAG` and `lang=TAG` ARE THE SAME KEY, so writing
   both is a repeated key and nothing more. RENDER ORDER above already decides
   it: the LAST value wins and the slot stays at the FIRST appearance. That
   holds inside one block and across merged block-attribute lines, because the
   shorthand desugars before either merge sees it. So `{:fr lang=de}` and
   `{lang=de :fr}` are `lang="de"` and `lang="fr"`; `{k=1 :fr lang=de}` is
   `k="1" lang="de"`, with `lang` in the slot `:fr` opened. This clause adds no
   rule -- it records that the shorthand needed none, which is the whole claim
   of "exact sugar" and is worth stating where a reader would otherwise look
   for a special case.

   CANONICAL FORM: the writer COLLAPSES a repeated `lang` to the one attribute
   that survives the merge, and spells it `:TAG` -- `{:fr lang=de}` formats to
   `{:de}`. An empty value is `{:}`, which is therefore the canonical spelling
   of `{lang=""}` too. Both follow from PART 11's rule that the writer emits
   the canonical spelling of what the AST holds, and the AST holds one `lang`.
   The `.fmt` byte fixture that pins this lands with the engine bump: those
   fixtures are read THROUGH the pinned build, which does not know the
   shorthand yet, and `tests/corpus-fmt-roundtrip.test.mjs` has no drift valve
   for them.

   A BOOLEAN `lang` IS THE SAME KEY AS WELL: `{lang}` means `lang=""` under
   PART 4's boolean rule, so `{:fr lang}` ends at `lang=""` and `{lang :fr}` at
   `lang="fr"`. This paragraph carried a caveat until carve#1125: the executable
   spec used to emit a boolean unconditionally, so `{a=1 a}` came out as
   `a="1" a=""` where every engine gave `a=""`. That is fixed, the three
   spellings of the key now meet in one slot everywhere, and the corpus pins the
   boolean form beside the other two rather than leaving it to prose. *)
language_attribute = ':', [ language_tag ] ;
language_tag       = language_subtag, { '-', language_subtag } ;
language_subtag    = ( letter | digit ), { letter | digit } ;
(* One to eight characters per subtag. *)

id_attribute = '#', explicit_identifier ;
class_attribute = '.', explicit_identifier ;
key_value_attribute = identifier, '=', attribute_value ;
(* A bare identifier with no value is a BOOLEAN (value-less) attribute,
   rendered `name=""` (a carve extension beyond canonical djot, matching
   djot-php; matched after key_value_attribute so `k=v` is not read as a bare
   `k`). It mixes freely with the other forms. See PART 9 §14. *)
boolean_attribute = identifier - ('_', {character}) ;
(* A FEW BOOLEAN NAMES ARE CONSUMED RATHER THAN EMITTED -- the attribute
   vocabulary, listed once so a reader looking for "which names mean something"
   finds them from the production rather than from three separate sections:

     loose         on a LIST or a DEFINITION LIST: the container's children
                   render as blocks rather than inline runs (PART 9 §17 L7)
     header-rows   on a PIPE TABLE, with `footer-rows`: the leading and
     footer-rows   trailing row ranges become `thead` / `tfoot`, boolean
                   meaning one (PART 12 §15)

   `aligns`, `valigns` and `widths` (PART 12 §19) are the same class in
   key/value form. Every other name reaches the output as the attribute it is,
   and each of these does too on a block that has nothing to consume it: the
   name is not reserved, it is read by the block below it. *)
(* A BOOLEAN ATTRIBUTE DOES NOT START WITH AN UNDERSCORE -- NORMATIVE
   (carve#1450). `identifier` admits a leading `_`, so `{_x_}` parsed BOTH as
   the boolean attribute `_x_` and as a forced underline. Alone on a line the
   attribute reading won and the underline was unreachable, and with no block
   beneath it to attach to, the line rendered NOTHING - five characters kept
   in the source and gone from the output.

   The underline is what an author writing `{_x_}` means, so the BARE form
   gives the collision up. There is no boolean attribute to lose: HTML's set
   is `disabled`, `checked`, `readonly`, `required`, `hidden` and their kind,
   and not one of them starts with `_`.

   ONLY THE BARE FORM IS NARROWED. `{#_id}`, `{._class}` and `{_k=1}` all keep
   their leading underscore, and none of them can be read as an underline
   because none of them ends `_}`. The one underscore attribute in the wild,
   hyperscript's `_="on click …"`, is a key/value and is unaffected.

   The cost is `{_foo}`, which was a boolean attribute and is now text. It has
   no underline reading either, so it renders literally rather than becoming
   something else. *)

attribute_value = unquoted_value | quoted_value ;
unquoted_value = (letter | digit | '-' | '_' | '.' | ':')+ ;
(* `.` and `:` are admitted so common unquoted values -- version strings
   (`v1.2`), paths/data values (`a.b`), and namespaced/pseudo tokens
   (`xml:lang`, `sm:hover`) -- need no quoting. A value containing any OTHER
   non-identifier character must be quoted. *)
(* A backslash escapes ASCII punctuation inside a quoted value (same
   `escaped_char` rule as inline text), so a value can contain a literal
   quote: `"a\"b"` -> the value `a"b`. *)
quoted_value = '"', { escaped_char | (character - '"' - '\' - newline) }, '"'
             | "'", { escaped_char | (character - "'" - '\' - newline) }, "'" ;
(* A QUOTED VALUE STOPS AT THE NEWLINE -- NORMATIVE (carve#888).
   `block_attributes` reads the same `quoted_value`, so a line break inside
   a quoted value ends that block too. A block attribute may still span
   lines: `continuation` is where a newline is admitted, and it sits
   BETWEEN tokens, never inside one. *)

(* Block-level attributes appear on their own line(s) preceding a block.
   A single attribute block may span multiple physical lines (the `}`
   need not be on the opening line): a continuation is a single line
   break optionally followed by indentation. A BLANK line (two line
   breaks) ends the block -- it is not interior padding; an attribute
   block interrupted by a blank line is not a block_attributes (both
   reference impls treat it as literal text). Merge + reach semantics
   (including float-across-blank-lines BETWEEN separate blocks): §15. *)
block_attributes = '{', opt_pad, attribute, {attr_separator, attribute}, opt_pad, '}', newline ;
(* THE PADDING ADMITS A LINE BREAK TOO, not only the separator. This used
   `opt_ws` on both sides, which is spaces and tabs alone, so `{` + newline
   + `.a}` and `{.a` + newline + `}` were outside the production while the
   interior `{.a` + newline + `.b}` was inside it. All three engines accept
   every one of the three, and the paragraph below takes `class="a"` or
   `class="a b"` accordingly. The prose above the production already said a
   block "may span multiple physical lines (the `}` need not be on the
   opening line)" - the padding spelling was narrower than the sentence
   describing it (carve#878). The INLINE `attributes` production does not
   follow: it pads with `attr_pad = {space}` (carve#906), which admits neither
   a tab nor a line break, and an inline block does not span lines in any
   engine. *)
(* block_attributes requires AT LEAST ONE attribute -- there is NO block-level
   blessed-empty form (unlike the inline `[text]{}` span, which is blessed).
   A bare `{}` line is therefore NOT a block-attribute block; it stays a
   literal paragraph (`<p>{}</p>`). *)
attr_pad = {space} ;                     (* INLINE attribute-block padding:
                                            spaces only, no tab and no line
                                            break -- it sits inline *)
opt_ws = {whitespace} ;                  (* spaces/tabs only, no line breaks.
                                            BLOCK-LEVEL ONLY since carve#906:
                                            read by opt_pad, attr_separator
                                            and continuation, never by the
                                            inline `attributes` production *)
opt_pad = opt_ws, [continuation] ;       (* block-attribute padding: spaces/tabs,
                                            then AT MOST ONE line break plus its
                                            indentation -- a blank line ends the
                                            block and is never interior padding *)
attr_separator = (whitespace | continuation), opt_ws ;  (* one ws OR one line break *)
continuation = newline, opt_ws ;         (* a single line break + indent; NOT a blank line *)
